# PDT deployment settings — the non-secret half.
#
# Copy to `.env` at the repository root and fill in what your deployment
# needs. `.env` is gitignored; this file is committed and must never hold
# a real credential. Every setting here is optional: the loader
# (`backend/env_config.py`) starts without any of them, and a variable
# already exported in your shell wins over the line in this file.
#
# What does NOT belong here, and where it goes instead:
#
#   * **A provider API key.** Nothing in this project reads one from this
#     file. The value is supplied at runtime by whatever provider layer a
#     deployment runs — `backend/cc_switch.py` is the integration this
#     codebase ships, and it reads one provider row at a time — and the
#     subprocess is then launched pointed at that row's endpoint. A
#     deployment with no provider routing runs no routed provider; it
#     does not fall back to a key in this file. `env_config` used to
#     *require* one here, which meant any deployment whose credentials
#     were supplied the supported way was still refused a start. That
#     demand, and two more names that no code in this repository has ever
#     read, were removed 2026-10-08.
#   * **The two provider secrets** (Feishu app secret, Telegram bot
#     token). Read from the macOS keychain; see below.

# ── Optional: timezone ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Used for the timestamps on plan cards and verification reports.
# TIMEZONE=Asia/Shanghai

# ── Optional: Provider secrets — the index stays here, the secret does not ────
# Two of the keys below are credentials: the Feishu app secret and the
# Telegram bot token. They are read from the macOS keychain, not from this
# file, and each one is handed to the processes that need it over a file
# descriptor — a pipe the child already inherited — so its value appears in
# no environment, not this one and not any child process's. The two other
# keys stay in this file: they are the index, the `account` a keychain item
# is stored under, and an index is not a credential.
#
# The read is one lookup by index and nothing else:
#   security find-generic-password -a <index> -w ~/Library/Keychains/runtime-secrets.keychain-db
# The keychain is named rather than left to "whichever one is unlocked",
# and the one named is a keychain of this project's own. The login
# keychain is not used: it holds every credential this account has ever
# saved, so anything able to enumerate it enumerates these secrets
# alongside all the others, and looking an item up by account is not a
# boundary. An item filed anywhere else is not found, which is the usual
# reason a correctly configured keychain read still comes back empty.
#
# To add an item, once the index is set in this file, run these two:
#   security default-keychain -s ~/Library/Keychains/runtime-secrets.keychain-db
#   security add-generic-password -a <index> -s <label> -w   # prompts for the value
# The first command is what makes the second one put the item where the
# read looks. Left alone, `add-generic-password` files an item in the
# *default* keychain, and it cannot be redirected by writing the path
# after `-w`: `-w` takes the next word as the password, so the path
# silently becomes your secret and the item lands in the keychain you
# meant to avoid. That is the one way to end up with a keychain that is
# configured correctly and a secret that is still reported missing.
# The value is prompted for rather than passed on the command line,
# because a password typed on a command line lands in shell history,
# which is what the keychain is here to avoid.
# Restore your default keychain when you are done:
#   security default-keychain -s ~/Library/Keychains/login.keychain-db
# The `-s` label is required by the tool on this platform and is for your
# own bookkeeping; the lookup does not use it, so two items sharing a
# label are still told apart by their account.
#
# If your keychain is not at the path above, name it here rather than
# editing the code — relative paths are resolved against your home
# directory, absolute paths are used as written. Left unset, the read
# uses ~/Library/Keychains/runtime-secrets.keychain-db.
# PDT_KEYCHAIN_PATH=Library/Keychains/your-keychain-name.keychain-db
#
# The switch below decides whether the keychain is consulted at all, and
# its direction is the opposite of what the name suggests: the name says
# DISABLE, and the keychain is off by default. Unset — or any value other
# than 0 or false, including 1, true, and a typo — means the plaintext
# variables in this file are read, which is the behaviour that predates
# the keychain. Set it to 0 or false to read the keychain instead. With
# the keychain path on, a missing item is reported as missing: the
# plaintext variable is not used as a second try, because falling back to
# it is the exposure this path exists to remove.
#
# macOS only. Linux and Windows have no `security` tool, so those
# installations read the plaintext variables in this `.env` whatever the
# switch says, and their behaviour is unchanged by any of the above.
# PDT_DISABLE_KEYCHAIN_SECRETS=0

# ── Optional: Feishu card notifications ────────────────────────────────
# Leave these unset to run the workflow with no card notifications.
# Create a custom app in the Feishu/Lark developer console and grant it
# `im:message` before using these.
#
# Two different kinds of key. FEISHU_APP_ID is the index — the `account`
# the keychain item is stored under — and it stays in this file.
# FEISHU_APP_SECRET is the credential, read from the keychain; the line
# below is there so the name is findable, not so it can be filled in.
# FEISHU_APP_ID=cli_your_app_id_here
# FEISHU_APP_SECRET=your-app-secret-here
#
# Where cards are pushed. FEISHU_NOTIFY_CHAT_ID overrides the default;
# there is deliberately NO hardcoded fallback, so leaving both unset
# disables pushes rather than sending them somewhere unintended.
# FEISHU_DEFAULT_CHAT_ID=your-chat-id-here
# FEISHU_NOTIFY_CHAT_ID=your-chat-id-here

# ── Optional: Telegram mirror ──────────────────────────────────────────
# The same cards, mirrored to a Telegram chat through
# `backend/notifications/telegram_client.py`. Both values must be
# available before anything is sent; with either missing the channel is
# skipped (and reported as disabled, not as failing).
#
# Two different kinds of key. TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID is the index — the
# `account` the token's keychain item is stored under — and it stays in
# this file. TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN is the credential, read from the
# keychain; the line below is there so the name is findable, not so it
# can be filled in. See "Provider secrets" above for the lookup and the
# switch.
# TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=your-chat-id-here
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=your-bot-token-here
# Per-plan override, same shape as FEISHU_NOTIFY_CHAT_ID_<plan_id>:
# TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID_<plan_id>=your-chat-id-here